<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder from PM - Expect crit within 24 hours]</span></p>
<p><sub>my favorite kind of placeholder is the one that's only up for an hour</sub></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">The&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The SCP is a full functional space station, made out of wasted materials and it builds more modules if it collects more wasted materials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, a space station made of junk. Hm, it's fine? It's not particularly gripping to me, but it's not something I'd downvote based on. With a strong story, it could be good.</p>
<p>So, looking at your narrative, it's lacking in a progression. You've got a narrative premise, but not what happens next. It's lacking detail and a strong arc to keep the reader engaged. To address the narrative premise first:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A group of people wanted to create a breakaway civilisation, due to political difficulties, and considering they didn't have much, they wanted to see if a space station made out of garbage was possible</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't very interesting to me. &quot;They wanted to&quot; isn't a particularly relatable or engaging motivation, so the reader has no incentive to care about why their doing it, or to be engaged in the creation for another reason.</p>
<p>You have, however, given yourself a small hook to build more onto. &quot;Due to political difficulties&quot;. What are these difficulties? What's interesting about them? What happens amongst these people? And most importantly, how is this going to be reflected in their creation?</p>
<p>Here's my recommendation - change it so this is instead a group of people who created a space station/ship of some sort in order to escape political discord. Something happened that meant they abandoned it, and it began to build itself anomalously. From here, you can build a narrative that explores what these political difficulties were, why it caused these people to leave, and how this is reflected in the creation itself.</p>
<p>What you have right now is lacking in the necessary detail, but if you were to use the hook that is present and build on that, I think you could build a compelling narrative.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I hope this feedback is helpful. Feel free to PM me again if you want me to take another look after you've developed the narrative here.</p>

