<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">[Placeholder from PM]</span></p>
<p>My apologies for the delay in getting to you.</p>
<p>Given the length of time since my previous critique, I'm going to be treating this as effectively a new idea and not making comparisons to the previous version.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Critique</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The core here is fairly solid. It presents a mystery that gives the plot some impetus, which is a stronger opening than many concepts get. My issue lies in that the extrapolation from this mystery is very plain and entirely unexciting. There's the mystery of who the crew are, the Foundation concludes they are the creators of the space station. There's a mystery of how the space station was created, the Foundation concludes that the crew members created it. There's a mystery over where the crew comes from, why they built the space station, etc., the Foundation decides this is worth investigating. Nothing here is unexpected, nor is it particularly engaging as payoff on a mystery. It's too predictable.</p>
<p>Additionally, there's a lack of detail on the latter half of your story. You describe the Foundation begins to investigate where the crew came from, why they built the ship, etc., but you don't actually answer any of these questions in your pitch nor do you describe the events of their investigation and (presumably) their discovery. This is the kind of detail we really need, as the latter half of a narrative can make or break it, and gaining an understanding of the full picture is important to making sure that the concept as a whole is viable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By the end of the article, instead of knocking the scp out of orbit, the foundation decides to put the scp to use for research purposes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This isn't a huge issue in and of itself, but it's indicative of other issues. On it's own, this isn't interesting enough. It's a payoff without a setup; why should the reader care whether the Foundation decides to use this space station for their own purposes? Unless it's been established that the reader should care about this, they probably won't, and therefore this ending will feel very flat and un-satisfying to most readers. As an ending it's not momentous or shocking enoughto justify itself without a strong setup, but it also lacks that setup, hence it just feels flat.</p>
<p>I'd really recommend putting some thought into what the emotion and ideas you want to convey to the reader here are. There's a distinct lack of this beyond &quot;general mystery&quot; and it means the concept as a whole lacks depth. Additionally, consider what you want the reader to latch onto here. What's the thing that's going to keep them reading? Why are they going to care about the story? Will it be the characters? The mystery? Something else? A finer focus in that regard would greatly benefit this concept.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

