<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> The SCP is a full functional space station, made out of space debris and it builds more modules if it collects more space debris.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Love it! Nice and simple setting! Fantastic!</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The foundation decides to explore the SCP up close, revealing that there is a crew on board. The foundation attempts to contact them to ask questions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is pretty standard location anomaly stuff. Establish contact and determine properties. But there are many ways to add this in your article. What are you planning to do to make this an important part of the article? Or is this just setting up the rest?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The crew members don't exactly remember how they got there or anything about the station, only telling the things they DO remember.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What kinds of things DO they remember that will help the Foundation and the reader figure things out?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The foundation concludes by what the members have said that they are the creators of the space station who erased their own memories.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a weird thing for them to do and an especially weird thing to be able to figure out from interviews. Why do you think they erased their memories? What clues let the Foundation and the readers figure this out actively while moving through your world?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>After the conclusion was made, they decide to try to figure out where the crew came from, where and how they built and launched the station without anyone noticing, etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do they do that? What interesting details and hints are you going to go through? Is this DNA testing and more interviews or is it more like tracking movements and looking at missing persons? What are you planning to make this mysterious but also not confusing or vague?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By the end of the article, instead of knocking the scp out of orbit, the foundation decides to put the scp to use for research purposes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Making SCiPs useful can be a bit of a trap. What exactly makes this anomaly useful for research above a station of their own design and knowledge? That's like finding a popsicle on the ground (but it looks clean) and you decide to eat that one instead of the one in your hand already. There needs to be some logic behind using the unknown object that they don't fully understand and wasn't designed specifically for them instead of something they control. Money isn't <em>really</em> an issue for the Foundation (unless you want it to be) so think about the rationale.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Getting to explore a few new characters in an unusual setting, slowly solving a mystery in the process</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You've told me about your mystery (the origins and such) but not about your characters. If this is your hook, can you tell me more about them?</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I am completely lost at what I should do with the story, so feel free to make suggestions.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have some Ideas I might propose to you but first I would love to see how you answer these questions. It is your story first and foremost :)</p>
</div>
